 ## Overview
 
-The absorption-only model is a simplified volumetric light transport method that accounts exclusively for the attenuation of light as it passes through a participating medium, ignoring both emission from the volume and scattering [S1, S2, S3]. It solves the problem of rendering a scene where the volume acts as a "cold black" occluder, similar to black smoke or X-ray attenuation, by computing the radiance reaching the camera from emissive surfaces after it has been exponentially decayed by the medium's density [S1, S4]. Unlike the [Emission-Absorption Model](927-emission-absorption-model.md), which adds light generated within the volume, this model assumes the volume itself contributes no radiance, making it computationally cheaper but visually limited to opacity-based visualization [S2, S4].
+The absorption-only model is the simplest participating medium model, accounting exclusively for the attenuation of light as it passes through a volume while ignoring emission from the medium and scattering [S2, S3]. It computes the radiance reaching the camera from emissive surfaces after exponential decay by the medium's density, effectively simulating a cloud of black smoke or X-ray attenuation [S1, S4]. Unlike the [Emission-Absorption Model](927-emission-absorption-model.md), which adds light generated within the volume, this model assumes the volume contributes no radiance and surfaces only emit light without reflecting or transmitting it [S1, S2].
 
