 ### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Extinction vs. Voxel Opacity**: Do not confuse the extinction coefficient $\tau$ with voxel opacity $\alpha$. $\alpha$ depends on the path length through the voxel, which varies if the voxel is scaled or viewed diagonally. $\tau$ is a local property (differential opacity per unit length) [S3].
-*   **Homogeneity Assumption**: The simple closed-form solution $L = e^{-\sigma_a t} L_e$ relies on $\sigma_a$ being constant. If $\sigma_a$ varies spatially, the integral $\int_0^s \tau(t) dt$ must be evaluated numerically along the ray [S3, S4].
-*   **Monochromatic vs. Color**: The model is often described as monochromatic (single $\sigma_a$ for all channels), but $\sigma_a$ can be a vector (RGB) to simulate colored absorption, while the surface $L_e$ provides color [S1].
+*   **Extinction vs. Voxel Opacity**: Do not confuse the extinction coefficient $\tau$ with voxel opacity $\alpha$. $\alpha$ depends on the path length through the voxel, which varies if the voxel is scaled or viewed diagonally [S3].
+*   **Homogeneity Assumption**: The simple closed-form solution $L = e^{-\sigma_a t} L_e$ relies on $\sigma_a$ being constant [S1].
+*   **Spatial Variation**: For spatially varying density, the integral $\int_0^s \tau(t) dt$ must be evaluated continuously along the viewing ray [S3].
+*   **Monochromatic Assumption**: The basic model assumes a single $\sigma_a$ for all color channels, though $L_e$ can still be colored [S1].
 
